n='nsec-threats'
edit(n,'The protocols in the earlier modules were designed for a small, friendly network of universities. IP, Ethernet, ARP, DNS and UDP all assume that whoever sends a packet is telling the truth about who they are. None of them encrypts anything.','Basic IPv4, ARP, UDP and traditional DNS do not by themselves cryptographically authenticate every sender or encrypt application data. Checksums detect some accidental corruption, but do not prove identity. Security extensions and deployment controls can add protections.','Remove inaccurate shared historical origin and universal absence of protocol/security extensions.')
edit(n,'That trust is the root of almost every network attack.','Missing authentication and encryption enable several important network attacks.','No evidence for almost-every prevalence claim.')
edit(n,'Wi-Fi is radio: anyone in range receives every frame.','Wi-Fi is radio: a nearby receiver may capture frames on a monitored channel, subject to signal, interference and radio capabilities.','Being nearby does not guarantee receipt of every frame.')
edit(n,'A switch only sends a frame to the right port, but an attacker can flood its MAC table or poison ARP (covered in the foundations module) to pull traffic towards themselves.','A switch normally forwards known unicasts toward the learned destination port, while broadcasts and some unknown traffic are flooded. MAC-table exhaustion or ARP poisoning may expose additional traffic, depending on switch protections and topology.','Switch forwarding includes flooding; attack outcome not guaranteed.')
edit(n,'Almost every identifier on a network can be forged, because nothing checks it.','Many packet fields can be written by a sender. Whether a forgery is accepted depends on filtering, cryptographic validation and protocol state.','Remove blanket claim that nothing checks identifiers.')
edit(n,"Spoofing a full TCP connection is much harder because the attacker must guess the server's 32-bit initial sequence number, which modern stacks randomise.","An off-path attacker who cannot observe replies has an additional obstacle when forging a TCP handshake: the acknowledgment must match the server's initial sequence number plus one in the ordinary no-data handshake. Unpredictable sequence-number generation makes blind guessing harder.",'Scope off-path and ordinary handshake; on-path attackers can observe replies.',rfc(9293))
edit(n,"RFC 2827 (BCP 38) asks every network to drop outbound packets whose source address does not belong to that network. If every ISP did this, reflection attacks would be impossible. Many still do not, which is why reflection DDoS persists.","RFC 2827 (BCP 38) recommends filtering customer traffic against permitted source prefixes. This blocks spoofing outside those prefixes, but does not by itself stop one host impersonating another inside an allowed prefix. More granular source validation and reflector controls are also useful.",'RFC explicitly permits within-prefix spoofing; no absolute prevention.',rfc(2827))
edit(n,"**BGP hijacking**: announce someone else's prefix so the internet routes it to you.","**BGP hijacking**: an unauthorised announcement may redirect traffic in networks that accept and select it.",'Authorized transit also announces others prefixes; selection not universal.',rfc(6811))
edit(n,'**Nonces**: a random value per request; the server remembers which it has seen.','**Nonces**: bind a unique value to the authenticated request; atomically reject reuse in the relevant session or account.','Nonce must be authenticated and replay-state updates atomic.')
edit(n,'**Timestamps**: reject messages older than a few seconds (needs roughly synchronised clocks).','**Timestamps**: an authenticated timestamp can limit the acceptance window, but a replay inside that window still needs duplicate detection. Clock and expiry rules must be defined.','Timestamp alone does not enforce once-only execution.')
edit(n,'**Sequence numbers**: each message carries a counter; the receiver rejects anything not newer than what it has seen. IPsec, TLS and WireGuard all do this (lesson 4 shows the sliding window).','**Sequence numbers**: authenticate counters and track accepted values. An ordered channel can require the next counter; an IPsec-style sliding window can accept unseen out-of-order packets while rejecting duplicates and packets that are too old. TLS early data has separate replay caveats (lesson 3).','Sliding-window protocols allow unseen older packets; TLS early data exception.',rfc(4303)+rfc(8446))
edit(n,'knocked the DNS provider Dyn offline and with it many large websites.','disrupted DNS resolution at Dyn and access to many customer websites.','Avoid imply all Dyn and customer servers physically offline.',['https://www.usenix.org/system/files/conference/usenixsecurity17/sec17-antonakakis.pdf'])
edit(n,'with far more capacity than any attacker.','with sufficient capacity and filtering for the particular attack.','No provider has guaranteed capacity exceeding every attacker.')
edit(n,"The server stores nothing on receiving a SYN; instead, it encodes the connection details in the initial sequence number of its SYN-ACK. A genuine client's ACK echoes that number back, and the server rebuilds the state from it.","For a SYN handled with a cookie, the server avoids retaining a per-request half-open connection entry. It encodes limited information in the SYN-ACK's initial sequence number; the client's acknowledgment is that number plus one. The server validates the cookie and reconstructs connection state. Shared keys, counters and transient packet processing still exist.",'Correct ACK plus one and per-request state scope.',rfc(4987))
edit(n,'The most effective volumetric attacks combine spoofing with **reflection** and **amplification**:','One important family of volumetric attacks combines spoofing with **reflection** and **amplification**:','No universal effectiveness ranking evidence.')
edit(n,'**Headers dilute the factor.** Every packet carries about 28 bytes of IPv4 and UDP headers (more with Ethernet framing).','**Headers dilute the factor in this unfragmented example.** A UDP datagram carried in one IPv4 packet with no options has 28 bytes of IP and UDP headers; link-layer overhead is additional.','Fragmentation/options invalidate universal28bytes.')
edit(n,'but on the wire it is (1,400 + 28) / (40 + 28) = 21.','but counting only IP and UDP headers the ratio is (1,400 + 28) / (40 + 28) = 21. A full link-level ratio also needs framing and packetisation.','IPbyte ratio is not fullwire ratio.')
edit(n,"**Protocol design**: newer protocols refuse to amplify unauthenticated peers. QUIC limits a server to sending three times what it has received until the client's address is validated.","**Protocol design**: cap amplification before validating a peer's address. QUIC permits up to three times the received UDP payload bytes before address validation; this bounds amplification rather than eliminating it.",'3x explicitly remains amplification.',rfc(9000))
edit(n,'Core internet protocols trust whatever the sender claims, so addresses, ARP and DNS can all be forged.','Basic packet identifiers are not cryptographic identities; validation and deployment controls determine which forgeries are accepted.','Align qualified introductory claim.')
edit(n,'Replay is defeated by nonces, timestamps or sequence numbers, not by signatures alone.','Replay protection needs authenticated freshness data and correct receiver state. A timestamp alone only limits the replay window.','Fix timestamp-alone false takeaway.')
edit(n,'and BCP 38 plus closing reflectors is the cure.','and source validation plus reflector controls reduce the available attack paths.','No absolute cure guarantee.')
qedit(n,6,'answer',[0,4],'Timestamp-only answer incorrectly marked sufficient against replay.')
qedit(n,6,'options.2.explanation','Incorrect as a complete replay defence: the attacker can resend within the accepted time window. A timestamp limits age but still needs authenticated duplicate detection.','Make timestamp distractor consistent with corrected answer.')
qedit(n,6,'options.4.explanation','Correct if the counter is authenticated, scoped to the session and acceptance state is updated atomically. Strictly increasing acceptance rejects the repeated counter; sliding-window protocols instead track duplicates while tolerating some reordering.','Do not identify strict counter with IPsec slidingwindow.',rfc(4303))
qedit(n,6,'workedExample','A duplicate must be distinguishable through authenticated freshness data and receiver state.\n\n| Measure | Identical replay rejected? |\n|---|---|\n| Recorded authenticated nonce | Yes, including inside the time window |\n| Stronger signature alone | No; original signature remains valid |\n| Timestamp alone | Only after expiry, not within the window |\n| Encryption alone | No; the same protected bytes can be replayed |\n| Authenticated increasing session counter | Yes, with atomic acceptance state |\n\nNeither stronger encryption nor a valid signature by itself guarantees freshness.','Correct contradictory worked replay table.')
qreplace(n,6,'prompt','Which measures stop the attacker replaying it?','Assume added freshness fields are authenticated and receiver checks update state atomically. Which measures prevent an identical request from being accepted twice, even within a valid timestamp window?','Specify replay within window and binding assumptions.')
qreplace(n,2,'prompt','Why is IP','For an off-path attacker unable to observe replies to the forged address, why is IP','Scope necessary reply-visibility assumption.')
qreplace(n,3,'options.1.explanation','Mallory cannot present a valid certificate for the bank\'s name, so a proper check fails.','Assuming the trust store and bank credentials are uncompromised, Mallory cannot supply the required name-bound authentication proof, so verification fails.','Certificate alone insufficient and trust compromise possible.')
qreplace(n,3,'workedExample',"In TLS the server proves ownership of a certificate for the bank's name, signed by a trusted CA. Mallory has no such certificate.","In certificate-authenticated TLS, validate the certificate chain and bank name, and verify the server's proof of the corresponding private key. A copied public certificate alone is insufficient.",'Possession ofpubliccertificate notauthentication.',rfc(8446))
qreplace(n,7,'options.0.explanation','SYN cookies answer every SYN.','Cookies allow eligible new attempts without retaining a half-open entry; filtering and resource limits may still drop SYNs.','No guarantee answer everypacket.',rfc(4987))
qedit(n,7,'options.3.text','It avoids a per-request half-open entry and puts a verifiable cookie in the SYN-ACK initial sequence number','Global/transient state notzero.',rfc(4987))
qedit(n,7,'options.3.explanation','Correct. The acknowledgment contains cookie + 1. After validation the server can reconstruct connection state; this saves half-open entries but does not eliminate packet-processing costs.','Fix plusone/no-memory overclaim.',rfc(4987))
qreplace(n,7,'workedExample','With SYN cookies the server stores nothing.','For a cookie-handled SYN the server does not retain a per-request half-open entry.','Per-requestscope.',rfc(4987))
qreplace(n,7,'workedExample','which is why Linux only uses cookies when the queue overflows.','Linux tcp_syncookies=1 uses overflow fallback; value 2 requests unconditional cookie generation.','Kernel alsoallowsmode2.',['https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/networking/ip-sysctl.html'])
qreplace(n,8,'workedExample','It queues and drops about three quarters of everything, without knowing which packets are legitimate.','With a sustained 40 Gbps offered load and a 10 Gbps output, queues cannot absorb the excess indefinitely. Which packets are dropped depends on scheduling and upstream filtering; it is not necessarily three quarters of each traffic class.','Aggregatecapacitydoesnotimplyuniformdropfraction.')
qreplace(n,9,'options.0.explanation',"If spoofed source addresses never leave the attacker's network, reflectors cannot be aimed at a victim.","Filtering permitted source prefixes blocks external-prefix spoofing. Finer validation is needed to prevent impersonation within an allowed prefix.",'BCP38sameprefixlimitation.',rfc(2827))
qedit(n,9,'options.3.explanation','Correct. QUIC bounds pre-validation amplification at 3× received bytes. It reduces the amplification available to an attacker; it does not remove all reflection.','3xnotzero.',rfc(9000))
qreplace(n,9,'options.4.explanation',"Memcached's huge BAF made exposed servers the most dangerous reflectors; since 1.5.6 UDP is off by default.","The 1.5.6 release disables UDP by default, reducing exposure unless operators enable it. Danger also depends on reachable capacity and configuration, not BAF alone.",'Removeunsupportedmostdangerousranking.',['https://docs.memcached.org/releasenotes/releasenotes156/'])
qedit(n,9,'workedExample','Reflection amplification needs a response directed to the victim and an amplifying service.\n\n1. Source-prefix filtering limits who can be impersonated; within-prefix spoofing can remain.\n2. Closing unintended reflectors removes those endpoints from the available pool.\n3. QUIC bounds pre-validation amplification at 3×, rather than reducing it to zero.\n\nThese measures reduce systemic exposure. A victim-only firewall rate limit does not remove the reflectors or the upstream bandwidth bottleneck.','Replacefalseallthreeguarantees.',rfc(9000)+rfc(2827))
qreplace(n,10,'prompt','is padded to 1,200 bytes','has a UDP payload padded to 1,200 bytes','ClarifyQUICbyteaccounting.',rfc(9000))
qreplace(n,10,'workedExample',"or sends a Retry token, which proves the client can receive at that address.","or sends a Retry. Only receiving and validating the returned token establishes that the client could receive it; merely sending a Retry proves nothing.",'Retry send notproof.',rfc(9000))
qreplace(n,12,'prompt',"server's random initial sequence number", "server's initial sequence number, modeled as uniformly random across 32 bits for an ordinary no-data handshake",'Probability needsuniformmodel notanyrandomizedgenerator.',rfc(9293))
qedit(n,12,'options.1.explanation','Correct under the stated uniform model: one exact guess succeeds with probability 1/2^32. The receive-window tolerance for established data does not relax this ordinary handshake acknowledgment requirement.','Removeincorrectwindowtolerance.',rfc(9293))
